and we're steering our ship, our objectives,

521
00:20:57,420 --> 00:20:58,700
to match them.

522
00:20:58,700 --> 00:21:00,740
It signals market awareness,

523
00:21:00,740 --> 00:21:02,020
and it stops you from building

524
00:21:02,020 --> 00:21:05,860
a perfect, beautiful solution to a problem nobody actually has.

525
00:21:05,860 --> 00:21:07,780
The fastest way to go out of business.

526
00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:11,340
Okay, so the strategist is about looking at the big picture,

527
00:21:11,340 --> 00:21:13,740
the market, the volatility of the tech.

528
00:21:13,740 --> 00:21:16,860
But leadership isn't just about sunny days and big visions.

529
00:21:16,860 --> 00:21:18,780
It's also about the scary stuff,

530
00:21:18,780 --> 00:21:20,780
the things that can go wrong.

531
00:21:20,780 --> 00:21:23,300
This brings us to the prepared leader.

532
00:21:23,300 --> 00:21:24,780
This is a critical section.

533
00:21:24,780 --> 00:21:26,300
A huge part of the source material

534
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:29,700
focuses on caution, analysis, and looking before you leap.

535
00:21:29,700 --> 00:21:32,140
You cannot be a leader if you are a blind optimist.

536
00:21:32,140 --> 00:21:33,900
You have to be a risk manager.

537
00:21:33,900 --> 00:21:36,900
The first phrase here is comprehensive risk assessment.

538
00:21:36,900 --> 00:21:39,180
And the keyword there is comprehensive,

539
00:21:39,180 --> 00:21:42,300
not just a risk check, a comprehensive risk assessment.

540
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:43,700
What's the real difference?

541
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:45,660
A risk check is just asking your buddy,

542
00:21:45,660 --> 00:21:47,540
hey, do you think this will work?

543
00:21:47,540 --> 00:21:49,900
A comprehensive risk assessment

544
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:52,540
is a formal systematic process.

545
00:21:52,540 --> 00:21:55,700
It implies we are going to look under every single rock.

546
00:21:55,700 --> 00:21:57,340
We are going to check for legal risks,

547
00:21:57,340 --> 00:21:59,500
financial risks, technical risks,

548
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:01,240
and reputational risks.

549
00:22:01,240 --> 00:22:03,140
It sounds incredibly responsible.

550
00:22:03,140 --> 00:22:05,180
I know if I hear a manager say,

551
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:06,540
before we launch this,

552
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:08,900
we need a comprehensive risk assessment,

553
00:22:08,900 --> 00:22:10,060
I feel safer.

554
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:12,460
I feel like they aren't gonna drive the bus off a cliff.

555
00:22:12,460 --> 00:22:14,940
That is exactly the feeling you want to induce

556
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:16,860
in your team and your stakeholders.

557
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:19,220
I am not gambling with the company's money.

558
00:22:19,220 --> 00:22:21,940
I am calculating the odds like a professional.

559
00:22:21,940 --> 00:22:23,500
And closely related to that is,

560
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:25,540
conduct a thorough market analysis.

561
00:22:25,540 --> 00:22:27,340
Again, it's that adjective thorough

562
00:22:27,340 --> 00:22:28,860
that does all the heavy lifting.

563
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:30,460
We aren't just Googling for five minutes.

564
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:31,940
We are digging deep.

565
00:22:31,940 --> 00:22:33,260
This is about due diligence.

566
00:22:33,260 --> 00:22:35,700
It's about respecting the market enough to study it

567
00:22:35,700 --> 00:22:37,300
before you try to enter it.

568
00:22:37,300 --> 00:22:39,280
Now, these next two are my personal favorites

569
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:40,920
for when things are actually looking bad

570
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:42,620
or might look bad in the future.

571
00:22:42,620 --> 00:22:45,120
Gap analysis and contingency plan.

572
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:47,820
Oh, these are the problem solvers, two best friends.

573
00:22:47,820 --> 00:22:49,820
Let's start with gap analysis.

574
00:22:49,820 --> 00:22:52,620
It sounds so clinical and detached.

575
00:22:52,620 --> 00:22:54,500
That's exactly why it's brilliant.

576
00:22:54,500 --> 00:22:56,700
Let's say your team is failing.

577
00:22:56,700 --> 00:22:59,540
You are at 50% your quarterly sales goal.

578
00:22:59,540 --> 00:23:01,140
If you go into a meeting and say,

579
00:23:01,140 --> 00:23:03,140
we are failing, what happens?

580
00:23:03,140 --> 00:23:05,700
Everyone panics, blame starts getting thrown around,

581
00:23:05,700 --> 00:23:06,740
you look like a loser.

582
00:23:06,740 --> 00:23:07,900
But if you walk in and say,

583
00:23:07,900 --> 00:23:10,180
we need to conduct a gap analysis.

584
00:23:10,180 --> 00:23:11,900
Ah, it's different.

585
00:23:11,900 --> 00:23:15,640
It completely reframes failure as a math problem.

586
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:18,420
You have the current state, which is 50% of sales,

587
00:23:18,420 --> 00:23:19,860
and you have the desired state,

588
00:23:19,860 --> 00:23:21,900
which is 100% of sales.

589
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:24,400
The gap is the 50% in the middle.

590
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:27,440
The analysis is simply the process of identifying

591
00:23:27,440 --> 00:23:30,320
why that gap exists and how we can close it.

592
00:23:30,320 --> 00:23:32,600
So it takes all the emotion and shame out of failure.

593
00:23:32,600 --> 00:23:36,680
It's not we suck, it's there is a gap in our performance

594
00:23:36,680 --> 00:23:37,900
and we are now analyzing it.

595
00:23:37,900 --> 00:23:39,680
It transforms an emotional crisis

596
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:41,120
into a logistical puzzle.

597
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:43,520
It protects your ego and more importantly,

598
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:46,200
keeps the entire team focused on the solution,

599
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:47,240
not the problem.

600
00:23:47,240 --> 00:23:48,760
And what about contingency plan?

601
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:50,680
That is your plan B.

602
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:52,600
But calling it a plan B sounds like

603
00:23:52,600 --> 00:23:54,120
you've already given up on plan A.

604
00:23:54,120 --> 00:23:55,120
It sounds weak.

605
00:23:55,120 --> 00:23:56,120
Right.

606
00:23:56,120 --> 00:23:59,800
Calling it a contingency plan sounds like you are a chess master.

607
00:23:59,800 --> 00:24:02,040
You are thinking three moves ahead.

608
00:24:02,040 --> 00:24:05,840
If X happens, we will execute protocol Y.

609
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:07,880
It's proactive, not reactive.

610
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:09,800
It signals emotional maturity.

611
00:24:09,800 --> 00:24:10,800
It really does.

612
00:24:10,800 --> 00:24:12,480
It says, I am not afraid of things going wrong.

613
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:14,960
In fact, I am prepared for them.

614
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:17,080
It comforts your stakeholders.

615
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:20,760
They know that if the building catches fire, you're the one who knows where all the

616
00:24:20,760 --> 00:24:22,040
exits are.

617
00:24:22,040 --> 00:24:26,540
And the final phrase for the prepared leader is surprisingly humble, I think, revisiting

618
00:24:26,540 --> 00:24:28,540
and questioning our assumptions.

619
00:24:28,540 --> 00:24:30,880
This is phrase 74 in the source.

620
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:34,780
And I love this one because it requires real intellectual honesty.

621
00:24:34,780 --> 00:24:36,740
It sounds a bit like admitting you might have been wrong.

622
00:24:36,740 --> 00:24:40,420
It is admitting that circumstances might have changed and therefore our original assumptions

623
00:24:40,420 --> 00:24:42,700
may no longer be valid.

624
00:24:42,700 --> 00:24:45,780
We assumed the customer wanted this feature a year ago.

625
00:24:45,780 --> 00:24:49,300
We need to question that assumption before we spend another million dollars building

626
00:24:49,300 --> 00:24:50,300
it.

627
00:24:50,300 --> 00:24:51,460
So it encourages critical thinking.

628
00:24:51,460 --> 00:24:56,960
It gives the whole team permission to challenge the status quo without being labeled as troublemakers

629
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:58,120
or negative.

630
00:24:58,120 --> 00:25:00,640
It's the emperor has no clothes phrase, isn't it?

631
00:25:00,640 --> 00:25:01,640
Yeah.

632
00:25:01,640 --> 00:25:04,400
It allows someone on the team to say, hey, are we sure the emperor is actually wearing

633
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:07,840
pants without getting metaphorically beheaded?

634
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:09,240
That is a perfect analogy.

635
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:12,200
It creates psychological safety for the truth to come out.

636
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:13,400
All right, let's pivot.

637
00:25:13,400 --> 00:25:16,840
We've covered strategy, action, and risk.

638
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:20,880
But business at its core is mostly about people.

639
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:25,960
You can have the best contingency plan in the world, but if nobody likes you, or nobody

640
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:28,380
listens to you, it just doesn't matter.

641
00:25:28,380 --> 00:25:29,800
This is the diplomat section.

642
00:25:29,800 --> 00:25:34,620
This is all about managing up, managing down, and managing across the organization.

643
00:25:34,620 --> 00:25:39,240
The first phrase is one you hear everywhere, but I think people misuse it constantly.

644
00:25:39,240 --> 00:25:41,920
Engaging with stakeholders early on.

645
00:25:41,920 --> 00:25:42,920
Stakeholders.

646
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:44,120
That is the magic word.

647
00:25:44,120 --> 00:25:45,760
Who exactly is a stakeholder?

648
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:46,760
Is it just your boss?

649
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:49,760
No, and that's where so many people mess up.

650
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:52,640
A stakeholder is anyone with skin in the game.

651
00:25:52,640 --> 00:25:54,240
It's your boss, yes.

652
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:55,920
But it's also the finance team

653
00:25:55,920 --> 00:25:58,880
who has to approve the budget for your project.

654
00:25:58,880 --> 00:26:01,440
It's the IT guy who has to install

655
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:03,080
the software you wanna buy.

656
00:26:03,080 --> 00:26:06,240
It's the customer support rep who has to answer the calls

657
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:08,360
about the new product you're launching.

658
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:12,080
So the janitor could theoretically be a stakeholder

659
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:13,760
if the project involves, I don't know,

660
00:26:13,760 --> 00:26:15,400
a major office renovation.

661
00:26:15,400 --> 00:26:16,400
Absolutely.

662
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:19,280
And the phrase engaging with stakeholders early on

663
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:21,560
is all about political intelligence.

664
00:26:21,560 --> 00:26:23,360
If you build an entire project

665
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:25,360
and then show it to the IT guy at the very end

666
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:27,440
and he says, sorry, this isn't compatible

667
00:26:27,440 --> 00:26:29,920
with our security servers, you are dead.

668
00:26:29,920 --> 00:26:31,600
You've just wasted months of work.

669
00:26:31,600 --> 00:26:33,720
Months, but if you engaged with him

670
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:36,080
early in the process, you would have avoided that.

671
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:38,040
And more importantly, you made him feel respected

672
00:26:38,040 --> 00:26:39,000
and part of the process.

673
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:39,840
You got his buy-in.

674
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:42,640
Buy-in, that's another Q term from the source here.

675
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:45,000
Buy-in is the difference between compliance,

676
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:46,560
which is I have to do this

677
00:26:46,560 --> 00:26:49,120
because my boss said so, and commitment,

678
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:51,820
which is, I want to do this because I believe in it.

679
00:26:51,820 --> 00:26:53,960
Early engagement creates buy-in.

680
00:26:53,960 --> 00:26:55,960
That leads perfectly into the next phrase.

681
00:26:55,960 --> 00:26:58,520
Collaboration between departments is crucial.

682
00:26:58,520 --> 00:26:59,800
This is the official weapon

683
00:26:59,800 --> 00:27:01,720
in the fight against the silo mentality.

684
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:03,280
The silos, the invisible walls

685
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:04,360
between the sales department

686
00:27:04,360 --> 00:27:05,480
and the engineering department.

687
00:27:05,480 --> 00:27:06,320
Exactly.

688
00:27:06,320 --> 00:27:09,840
When a leader explicitly calls for collaboration,

689
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:12,040
you are giving people official permission

690
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:13,880
to tear down those walls.

691
00:27:13,880 --> 00:27:16,160
You are saying, we are not team sales

692
00:27:16,160 --> 00:27:17,320
versus team engineering.

693
00:27:17,320 --> 00:27:20,600
We are one team, team company.

694
00:27:20,600 --> 00:27:24,040
It frames the project as a potential shared victory,

695
00:27:24,040 --> 00:27:26,320
not a departmental competition.

696
00:27:26,320 --> 00:27:28,120
But how do you say that without it just sounding

697
00:27:28,120 --> 00:27:30,040
like a cheesy poster on the wall?

698
00:27:30,040 --> 00:27:32,040
Team work makes the dream work.

699
00:27:32,040 --> 00:27:34,480
You use the phrase in the context of dependency.

700
00:27:34,480 --> 00:27:36,600
You say, collaboration between departments

701
00:27:36,600 --> 00:27:38,680
is crucial on this project because sales

702
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:40,880
needs the technical data from engineering

703
00:27:40,880 --> 00:27:42,640
to close the deal.

704
00:27:42,640 --> 00:27:44,440
You explain why the silo needs to break

705
00:27:44,440 --> 00:27:45,920
for everyone to succeed.

706
00:27:45,920 --> 00:27:48,120
I see, you connect the dots for them.

707
00:27:48,120 --> 00:27:49,760
Now let's talk about culture,

708
00:27:49,760 --> 00:27:51,640
fostering a culture of innovation.

709
00:27:51,640 --> 00:27:53,400
Fostering, I love that word.

710
00:27:53,400 --> 00:27:54,880
It's a nurturing word.

711
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:58,120
It's not demanding innovation or requiring innovation,

712
00:27:58,120 --> 00:28:01,400
it's fostering it, like a garden or tending a garden.

713
00:28:01,400 --> 00:28:04,450
The source connects this to team building.

714
00:28:04,450 --> 00:28:06,770
It means it's not just about one brilliant idea,

715
00:28:06,770 --> 00:28:08,250
it's about creating the environment

716
00:28:08,250 --> 00:28:10,330
where brilliant ideas can happen organically.

717
00:28:10,330 --> 00:28:12,330
Right, you can't just order people to be brilliant.

718
00:28:12,330 --> 00:28:14,530
Hey team, be innovative by Friday.

719
00:28:14,530 --> 00:28:15,730
It doesn't work like that.

720
00:28:15,730 --> 00:28:20,370
you have to create a culture of innovation where it's safe to fail, where it's safe to experiment,

721
00:28:20,370 --> 00:28:23,810
and where wild ideas are encouraged, not shot down.

722
00:28:23,810 --> 00:28:28,930
So using this phrase tells your team, I care about the quality of the soil,

723
00:28:28,930 --> 00:28:30,610
not just the fruit it produces.

724
00:28:30,610 --> 00:28:36,530
That's a beautiful metaphor for it, and very closely related is cultivating a positive team

725
00:28:36,530 --> 00:28:37,170
culture.

726
00:28:37,170 --> 00:28:41,170
I want to highlight a specific word the source uses in the example here.

727
00:28:41,170 --> 00:28:44,930
It says that this is paramount for our collective success.

728
00:28:44,930 --> 00:28:45,490
Paramount.

729
00:28:45,730 --> 00:28:50,770
such a strong word. I feel like I'm climbing a mountain when I hear it. It means of highest

730
00:28:50,770 --> 00:28:56,370
importance. It means this is above everything else. When a leader says cultivating a positive

731
00:28:56,370 --> 00:29:01,090
team culture is paramount, they are planting a flag in the ground. They're making a statement.

732
00:29:01,090 --> 00:29:05,810
A very strong one. They are saying we will not sacrifice our people for the result. We will

733
00:29:05,810 --> 00:29:10,930
not tolerate toxic behavior no matter how much money that person brings in. So it sounds

734
00:29:10,930 --> 00:29:17,250
soft positive culture, but the word paramount makes it hard as a rock. It makes it a non-negotiable

735
00:29:17,250 --> 00:29:22,210
principle. Exactly. It's a perfect combination of empathy and authority. Okay, we coming down

736
00:29:22,210 --> 00:29:27,250
the homestretch now, we have built the culture, we have the strategy, we've assessed all the risks.

737
00:29:27,810 --> 00:29:32,770
Now we need to close. We need to show results. We need to prove we are worth our paycheck.

738
00:29:32,770 --> 00:29:36,130
This is the closer section. This is how you talk about the finish line and what you've

739
00:29:36,130 --> 00:29:41,250
accomplished. The first phrase here is, in my honest opinion, the scariest phrase in the

740
00:29:41,250 --> 00:29:46,450
entire English language for an employee to say out loud, I'll take the lead on this project.

741
00:29:46,450 --> 00:29:50,530
Phrase number 15 in the core list, it is the career maker.

742
00:29:50,530 --> 00:29:54,610
Why is it so terrifying? Because it's the moment you voluntarily step out of the herd.

743
00:29:55,170 --> 00:29:58,370
Most people want to be on the email thread, but they don't want to be the subject of the

744
00:29:58,370 --> 00:30:03,090
email thread. They want to be in the CC line. They're safe there. They're just an observer.

745
00:30:03,090 --> 00:30:08,850
But when you say, I'll take the lead, you are painting a giant target on your own back.

746
00:30:08,850 --> 00:30:17,170
If it fails, it is 100% on you. But if it succeeds, it's also on you. That is how you get promoted.

747
00:30:17,170 --> 00:30:22,050
You cannot move up in an organization if you never take the wheel. This phrase

748
00:30:22,050 --> 00:30:27,170
moves you instantly from passenger to driver. So how do you say it without sounding arrogant?

749
00:30:27,170 --> 00:30:31,490
Like move over peasants, I'm taking charge now. You frame it as an active service.

750
00:30:31,490 --> 00:30:35,330
I know everyone is swamped right now, so I'll take the lead on this project to ensure we hit our

751
00:30:35,330 --> 00:30:40,930
deadline. You are taking a burden off the team. You are being a hero, not a dictator.

752
00:30:40,930 --> 00:30:44,130
I like that. Framing leadership as a service. Yeah.

753
00:30:44,130 --> 00:30:48,050
But once you take the lead, you have to prove it worked. And that's where this next

754
00:30:48,050 --> 00:30:50,930
phrase comes in. Quantifying the impact.

755
00:30:50,930 --> 00:30:52,930
Quantifying. Putting a number on it.

756
00:30:52,930 --> 00:30:57,330
The source puts it so well. It's not enough to say it worked. We need to measure how

757
00:30:57,330 --> 00:31:02,640
much. This is the language of the C-suite. Feelings are soft. Numbers are hard. If you

758
00:31:02,640 --> 00:31:07,360
go to your boss and say, the customers really liked the new website update, that's nice.

759
00:31:07,360 --> 00:31:12,200
It's warm and fuzzy. But it's not concrete. It's not. But if you say, we quantified the

760
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:17,360
impact of the website update and customer retention is up 12% year over year, that

761
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:22,280
is undeniable. It makes you bulletproof. You can argue with, customers liked it.

762
00:31:22,280 --> 00:31:23,880
You can't argue with 12%.

763
00:31:23,880 --> 00:31:25,280
Precisely.

764
00:31:25,280 --> 00:31:26,880
Leaders speak in metrics.

765
00:31:26,880 --> 00:31:30,960
Which brings us right to Establish Performance Metrics.

766
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:32,280
Phrase 92.

767
00:31:32,280 --> 00:31:35,280
The old saying, if you can't measure it, you can't manage it.

768
00:31:35,280 --> 00:31:40,160
When you ask to establish performance metrics at the start of a project, you are bringing

769
00:31:40,160 --> 00:31:43,080
a level of rigor to the table that most people don't.

770
00:31:43,080 --> 00:31:47,560
You're asking, what does success actually look like, let's define it now, in numbers.

771
00:31:47,560 --> 00:31:49,040
It protects you, doesn't it?

772
00:31:49,040 --> 00:31:50,240
Ideally, yes.

773
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:55,120
If we all agree at the start that success is selling 100 units, and I deliver 100 units,

774
